{
 "summary": "Clear pending buffer bytes before invoking the underlying stream write in `BufferedWriter::flush()` to avoid re-attempting writes during exception unwinding. Explicitly call `flush()` within try-catch blocks in `BlockManager::WriteBlock` and `BlockManager::WriteBlockUndo` so disk write errors trigger clean fatal error handling instead of aborting the process.",
 "problem": "When block or undo writes fail due to disk issues (such as a full filesystem), `AutoFile::write_buffer()` throws an exception. Because `BufferedWriter` previously flushed pending bytes in its implicitly `noexcept` destructor, write failures invoked `std::terminate` instead of normal node storage error handling, and a failed explicit `flush()` would re-attempt the write upon destructor unwinding.",
 "evidence": [
  "Author report: 'my 1 TB benchmarking servers have recently been filling up, so I've been seeing these failures more often.'",
  "PR description provides a minimal reproduction script demonstrating that short writes previously resulted in process aborts rather than a clean exit with 'Failed to write genesis block'.",
  "The bug was introduced in merged PR #31551, which added `BufferedWriter`."
 ],
